From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 14:26:31 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] media: apple: jpeg: take NV12/NV16 in one buffer, keep
 CAPTURE size, fix drain

Three problems showed up with GStreamer's v4l2jpegenc on the J616s
(T6041); the same code runs on the A18 Pro (T8140).

The engine prefetches its input up to AJ_INPUT_END. With NV12M/NV16M the
luma and chroma planes are separate buffers, and the driver set
AJ_INPUT_END to the end of whichever plane lies higher. The engine then
read past the end of the lower plane into whatever followed it, and
faulted on the DART whenever that was unmapped: at 640x480 and 1280x720
too, where no MCU padding is involved. It only worked when the chroma
buffer happened to sit right below the luma buffer. The planes also
covered whole MCU rows (1088 rows at 1080p) in sizeimage, and GStreamer
places the chroma plane by those sizes, so it copied the frame to the
wrong offset and crashed or hung at 1080p.

Take NV12 and NV16 instead: one buffer with the visible luma rows, the
chroma rows straight after them at the same stride, and the chroma rows
up to the next MCU boundary at the end. AJ_INPUT_END is the end of that
buffer, so every read the engine makes is inside it. The luma rows of
the last MCU row read into the chroma rows, which are cropped from the
JPEG. bytesperline and the chroma offset are those any client computes
for NV12/NV16; only sizeimage carries the extra tail.

S_FMT on OUTPUT recomputed the CAPTURE size from the new raw size. A
client that sized CAPTURE first (GStreamer does, at the 1080p default)
and then set 1280x720 got smaller buffers from REQBUFS than the size it
had agreed, and refused them. Keep the CAPTURE size that S_FMT returned
as a floor when OUTPUT changes later, and honour a larger size a client
asks for on TRY/S_FMT(CAPTURE), bounded at 64 MiB.

ENCODER_CMD STOP chooses the last queued source buffer, and the IRQ
handler removes finished buffers, with no common lock: a job completing
while STOP ran could leave the queue just after STOP chose it, and no
CAPTURE buffer was ever flagged LAST. gst-launch -e at 4K, where a job
takes several milliseconds, waited for EOS forever. Serialise the
completion, STOP and CAPTURE QBUF on a per-context spinlock, send the
EOS event also when STOP completes at once or a late CAPTURE buffer is
returned as LAST, and clear the last-buffer-dequeued state on START so
a second stream drains too.

KUnit covers the raw layout (1080p, 720p, 480p, 4K, an odd size and
4:2:2) and the coded size floor and bound.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
(cherry picked from commit 2859a07857d73c6c6b05127735774fad200760e8)
